      "pageDescription": "Mapping an Individual X Account Footprint with Grok and Advanced Search",
      "evidenceText": "Dutch Osint Guy Nico on X: \"Mapping an Individual X Account Footprint with Grok and Advanced Search\" / X Article Log in Sign up Article Log in Sign up Conversation Dutch Osint Guy Nico @dutch_osintguy New X Article: how to map any public X account's online footprint with Advanced Search + Grok. Step by step, copy-paste queries, zero experience needed. And why YOU stay in (and on) the loop. Practice on @NASA . No magic. Just method. Mapping an Individual X Account Footprint with Grok and Advanced Search 10 min read Intro Often I get asked: “Nico, where do I even start when I need to understand one X account?” Not the whole internet. Not a network. One handle. People then jump straight into tools. Scrapers. Dashboards. Fancy graphs. I like tools — I use them every day. But a tool without a search plan is just noise with a nicer UI. OSINT means Open Source Intelligence: using information that is lawfully available to the public, then analysing it so you can answer a real question. X (the platform formerly called Twitter) is one of those public sources. Grok is the AI assistant built by xAI, available on grok.com and inside X. It can search live posts on X and help you summarise what an account talks about. This first tip in the series walks you through a replicable footprint of one public account: what they post who they talk to when they are active which posts travel what media they share We will use Advanced Search operators (special words you type into X’s search box) and Grok as an accelerator. We will not treat Grok’s answers as finished intelligence. If you remember one thing from this article, remember this: AI assists. You conclude. What you need before you click anything An X account so you can use search and Grok. A research question that is answerable. Bad: “Get me everything on this person.” Better: “What topics has @NASA posted about since 1 September 2026, and which other accounts does it reply to most in that window?” A public practice target. Today: @NASA . Swap in @targethandle for your own lawful case later. A note file (paper is fine). You will log every query and every claim you accept or reject. Ethics note: stick to public accounts and lawful purposes. Do not use this workflow to harass, stalk, or expose private individuals. If your organisation has a mandate and retention rules, follow them. Human validation first (read this before you trust Grok) Grok is useful because it can read a lot of public posts quickly. It is also an AI model. That means it can: Hallucinate (state something that is not in the sources) Miss context (skip the parent post in a reply chain) Misread sarcasm, slang, or another language Over-cluster (group posts that only look related) Overconfidently infer location, identity, or intent from weak signals So we use two complementary roles for the human analyst: Human IN the loop You check each AI output against the original posts and sources before you keep it. Practical rule: no cited post URL → the claim does not enter your notes as a finding. It stays a lead at best. Human ON the loop You oversee the whole investigation: scope, ethics, confidence language, and the final conclusion. Grok does not decide what “enough” evidence is. You do. Keep that checklist open while you work. I will point back to it in every step where Grok summarises or infers. Step 1 — Open the profile and read it yourself (no AI yet) Go to x.com and sign in. In the top search box, type NASA and open the verified institutional profile @NASA . Read, in this order: Display name and @handle Bio (the short text under the name) Joined date (on the profile; often under the bio) Following / Followers / Posts counts Pinned post (the post stuck to the top of the timeline, if any) Website link in the bio, if present Why it works: your first impression must come from primary source material, not from a model summary. If Grok later says “this account joined in 2019” and the profile says 2007, you will catch it because you looked. What you should see for @NASA (example check, Oct 2026): a long-running institutional account, high follower count, space/exploration messaging. Your numbers will differ by day — log what you see, with the date/time in Europe/Amsterdam. Pitfall: lookalikes and parody accounts. Confirm the handle spelling character by character. Prefer the official website link in the bio when you need identity corroboration. Verification: write one sentence in your notes: “Profile reviewed by hand on [date/time Europe/Amsterdam]. Handle @NASA . Bio quote: … Pinned post ID/URL: …” Step 2 — Optional: ask Grok for a first summary (then distrust it on purpose) Now that you have read the profile yourself, let Grok give you a quick first draft. On x.com, click Grok in the left menu (on the phone app, tap the Grok icon in the bottom bar). You can also go to grok.com . Start a new chat. Paste one of the prompts below, wait for the answer, and read it as a draft , not a report. Copy-paste prompts: Summarise the main topics @NASA has posted about in the last 30 days. List each topic with 2 example post URLs. Which accounts does @NASA reply to or mention most in the last 30 days? Give handles and example URLs. Say when you are unsure. Do not infer real-world location or private identity. Stick to what appears in public posts. IN the loop — how to verify this output: For every topic Grok names, run Step 3 searches below and open at least three matching posts. If you cannot find them, strike the topic from your notes or mark it “unverified lead.” ON the loop: decide whether “last 30 days” is even the right window for your question. If not, change the window before you continue. Pitfall: for small or brand-new accounts Grok may find very little, or fill the gaps with guesses. If it can't give you post links, fall back to Advanced Search only. Step 3 — Advanced Search: map what the account posts Advanced Search is X’s filtered search. You can open the form at x.com/search-advanced , or type operators (special keywords) straight into the search box. 3a. Their original posts in a date window Copy-paste into the X search box: from:NASA since:2026-09-01 until:2026-10-01 -filter:replies -filter:retweets Then click Latest (not Top) so you see chronological results. PieceMeaningfrom:NASAOnly posts authored by @NASAsince :2026-09-01On or after that date (UTC)until:2026-10-01Before that date (not inclusive)-filter:repliesHide replies-filter:retweetsHide reposts from:NASA means only posts written by @NASA since:2026-09-01 means on or after that date (UTC) until:2026-10-01 means before that date (that day itself isn't included) -filter:replies hides replies -filter:retweets hides reposts Why this combination works (replace the table): Reading the profile yourself gives you ground truth from the primary source from:, to: and @ separate what they post, who replies to them and who mentions them since: and until: keep your collection to a fixed window min_faves: and filter:media show you what spread and what's visual Grok gives you speed by summarising, clustering and suggesting next steps You handle validation, ethics, confidence and the final report What you should expect: a scrollable list of @NASA posts from September 2026 (UTC). Click several. Note themes by hand before you ask Grok. 3b. High-engagement posts from:NASA min_faves:500 since:2026-09-01 min_faves:500 means “at least 500 likes.” Raise or lower 500 to match the account’s normal reach. For a huge account like NASA, 500 is low; for a small account, try min_faves:5 or 10. 3c. Media-heavy posts (good for later image questions to Grok) from:NASA filter:media since:2026-09-01 or images only: from:NASA filter:images since:2026-09-01 3d. Language filter (when relevant) from:NASA lang:en since:2026-09-01 lang:en keeps English-detected posts. Other codes: nl, fr, de, es, etc. Pitfalls until: is exclusive (posts on that calendar day UTC may be missing — use the next day). Search does not show deleted or protected posts. Operators can change; if a query returns nonsense, simplify to from:NASA and add one operator at a time. Mobile often has no Advanced Search form — type operators into the search bar anyway. Verification: save the exact query string and the timestamp you ran it. Results change. Step 4 — Map interactions: to: and @ Replies directed at the account to:NASA since:2026-10-01 These are posts written as replies to @NASA . Mentions of the account (broader) @NASA since:2026-10-01 -from:NASA @NASA finds posts that mention the handle. -from:NASA removes NASA’s own posts so you see the outside conversation. Who does the account talk to? from:NASA since:2026-09-01 Scan for @otherhandle inside NASA’s own posts and replies. For a tighter reply view: from:NASA filter:replies since:2026-09-01 Why it works: footprint is not only broadcasting. Interaction edges (to:, mentions, replies) show the public conversation graph around the account. Grok prompt (after you have skimmed results yourself): Based only on public X posts, list the 10 accounts @NASA replied to most since 2026-09-01. For each, give two example post URLs. If you cannot support a name with URLs, omit it. IN the loop: pick every handle Grok lists. Confirm with: from:NASA ( @OtherHandle ) since:2026-09-01 If the search is empty, drop that handle. Do not “round up” in the AI’s favour. ON the loop: frequent replies to @Space_Station on a NASA account are expected institutional coordination — not a secret network. Context is your job. Step 5 — Activity patterns and timezone clues (not proof) Run: from:NASA -filter:replies -filter:retweets since:2026-10-01 Switch to Latest . Open 20–40 posts. Note the local time X shows you, and mentally convert a sample to UTC . Ask: are posts clustered in certain hours? Certain weekdays? Burst around events? Grok prompt: Using public posts from @NASA since 2026-10-01, describe posting-hour patterns in UTC. Provide example post URLs for each cluster. Do not claim a physical location from timestamps alone. IN the loop: timestamps are clues , not geolocation. Scheduled posts, shift teams, and global social desks break naive timezone conclusions. For @NASA , expect institutional scheduling — say so in your notes. Pitfall: never write “the operator lives in X country” from likes-timed charts alone. That is inference inflation. Step 6 — Use Grok on grok.com for clustering (with citations) Open grok.com and sign in. Start a new chat. Paste a scoped prompt. Example: You are assisting an OSINT analyst. Task: footprint @NASA for 1–30 Sep 2026 (UTC). 1) Cluster public posts into themes. 2) For each theme, give 3 post URLs from x.com. 3) List notable accounts mentioned or replied to, with URLs. 4) Flag uncertainty. 5) Do not invent posts. If you lack sources, say so. Read the answer. Click every URL. Grok’s X search can use keyword operators and semantic search under the hood; image understanding exists when enabled for media in posts. You still open the originals. Optional media follow-up (only after you opened the post yourself): Here is a public post URL: https://x.com/NASA/status/ <ID> Describe only what is visible in the attached media. Do not guess unseen context. Quote any on-image text. Replace <ID> with a real post id from your search (example from live checks: a September/October 2026 NASA status URL you copied yourself). IN the loop for media: AI vision misreads overlays and memes. Compare the description to what your eyes see. Note “AI said / I see.” Explain this post: on individual posts, X also offers contextual help via Grok (“Explain this post” / reply to @grok ). Use it for unfamiliar acronyms, then verify against primary reporting if the claim matters. Step 7 — Optional: Lists for monitoring (not magic) An X List is a curated timeline of accounts you choose. On X, open Lists from the left menu (desktop) or menu sheet (mobile). Create a list, e.g. Footprint-NASA-adjacent. Add @NASA and a few handles that repeatedly appear in from:NASA mentions (only public accounts relevant to your question). Use the list timeline to watch the cluster without the main Home algorithm. You can also search posts from list members with the list: operator when you have the list id — useful later, not required for Tip 1. Pitfall: a list is a monitoring aid. It is not evidence of collusion. Why this combination works PieceJobProfile readGround truth from the primary sourcefrom: / to: / @Authorship vs conversation vs mentionsince: / until:Contained collection windowmin_faves: / filter:mediaPrioritise what spread or what is visualGrokSpeed: summarise, cluster, suggest pivotsYouValidation, ethics, confidence, reporting OSINT is a mindset. The operators are just precise questions to the index. Grok is a fast junior analyst who never gets to sign the report. Pitfalls (print this next to your monitor) Wrong handle — parody and fan accounts exist. Top vs Latest — Top is engagement-ranked; use Latest for chronology. UTC vs local — date operators are UTC-oriented; be explicit in notes. Deleted posts — search will not resurrect them. AI fluent nonsense — polished language ≠ correct citation. Sarcasm & other languages — read the thread yourself. Engagement ≠ importance — a quiet reply can matter more than a viral poster. Scope creep — if you leave the research question, stop and rewrite the question. Private people — do not practice on non-public individuals for content you will publish. Operator drift — re-test operators; platform search changes. How to verify findings (end-to-end) Use this close-out before you write anything public or client-facing: Every factual claim → at least one live post or profile field you opened. Every Grok theme → 3 manual confirming posts (or demote to “unverified”). Every “key interactor” → confirmed with from: / to: search, not vibes. Timezone / location language → worded as hypothesis or dropped. Gaps logged — what you could not answer is still an answer. Devil’s advocate pass — ask: “What would falsify this?” Rest the notes — even 15 minutes helps you see overclaim. That is human in the loop (item checks) and on the loop (judgment, ethics, stop/go). Recap — your copy-paste kit Profile: open @NASA and read the bio, pinned post and counts by hand, then ask Grok for a first summary and verify it. Core searches: from:NASA since:2026-09-01 until:2026-10-01 -filter:replies -filter:retweets from:NASA min_faves:500 since:2026-09-01 from:NASA filter:media since:2026-09-01 to:NASA since:2026-10-01 @NASA since:2026-10-01 -from:NASA from:NASA filter:replies since:2026-09-01 Core Grok prompts: see Steps 2, 4, 5, and 6 — always demand URLs, always click them. Rule of the series: Grok and X Advanced Search make the collection faster. Your mindset makes it intelligence. What this tip intentionally does not cover (later tips) Group / network mapping across many accounts Topic or narrative tracking Event-centric timelines Deeper List OSINT and shared-follower analysis Cross-platform pivots off bio links Next tip will pick one of those lanes. Same rule set: public examples, copy-paste steps, human validation. Dutch Osint Guy Nico Dutch guy with Open Source Intelligence & Analysis skills • OSINT • Principal Instructor @ SANSInstitute • SVP of engineering & Chief Innovator @ shadowdragonio More Articles Join 39.3K followers X. It’s what’s happening From x.com 12:51 PM · Oct 27, 2026 · 3,217 Views 7 14 17 Log in or sign up for X See what’s happening and join the conversation Continue with phone Continue with Apple Continue with Google or Log in with username or email Relevant people Dutch Osint Guy Nico @ dutch_osintguy Follow Dutch guy with Open Source Intelligence & Analysis skills • OSINT • Principal Instructor @SANSInstitute • SVP of engineering & Chief Innovator @shadowdragonio What’s happening Show more",
