6 52.6% NY21 $2.67 $0.73 $1.12 $0.82 30.7 57.2% NV02 $2.27 $0.30 $0.07 $1.85 81.4 43.1% LA06 $2.12 $0.67 $1.45 68.5 0.0% OH01 $2.06 $0.50 $0.01 $1.42 68.6 70.6% CA40 $2.00 $1.43 $0.57 28.3 0% Totals include broadcast, local cable, connected TV (CTV), digital (including Meta, Google, Snapchat and X), network cable, radio, satellite, and network broadcast. *Total spending will be greater in some rows than the sum of candidate and group spending because the total also includes any spending on party coordinated ads with candidates. **This percentage is the Democratic percentage of total. The total includes spending on behalf of the Independent candidate. CITE SOURCE OF DATA AS: AdImpact with analysis by the Wesleyan Media Project. $7 Billion in Ad Spending This Cycle; Nearly $1.4 Billion of that in Last Two Weeks As the election nears a close, ad spending across platforms in House, Senate and gubernatorial races has passed $7.1 billion in the cycle to-date (Table 5). This includes nearly $3 billion in ad spending in Senate races and $2 billion in House races. Across platforms in the full cycle, a majority of spending (53 percent) was committed to broadcast television ads and about a quarter to ads on streaming and CTV. About 12 percent of ad spending was on ads on digital platforms such as Meta, YouTube, websites, and other social media. Outside groups have dominated congressional spending, with ads from groups totaling $3 billion across House and Senate campaigns. Groups have outspent candidates in Senate races by nearly 3:1 and by 2:1 in House races. Table 5: Spending by Office, Sponsor Type and Media Channel (1/1/25 to 10/25/26) Office Advertiser Type Local Broadcast (in $Ms) CTV (in $Ms) Digital (in $Ms) Local Cable (in $Ms) Other* (in $Ms) Total (in $Ms) Governor Candidate 717.42 384.3 170.3 145.69 42.06 1459.77 Group 367.6 202.06 79.79 83.98 17.57 751 Coordinated 66.72 34.83 5.4 8.06 0.81 115.82 Senate Candidate 321.44 183.8 162.9 48.3 19.34 735.76 Group 1059.11 478.18 219.17 142.8 80.02 1979.28 Coordinated 98.28 37.63 3.88 12.71 4.55 157.05 House Candidate 330.06 129.85 100.69 64.05 14.44 639.08 Group 669.58 180.73 151.79 101.37 27.89 1131.36 Coordinated 140.24 26.1 0.73 6.22 1.11 174.41 Total $3,770.46 $1,657.46 $894.65 $613.18 $207.79 $7,143.54 % 52.8% 23.2% 12.5% 8.6% 2.9% Other includes network cable, radio, satellite, and network broadcast CTV includes spending on streaming, CTV devices and in-app ads. Digital includes spending on Meta, Google, Snapchat, and X CITE SOURCE OF DATA AS: AdImpact with analysis by the Wesleyan Media Project. Figure 1: Advertising Spending by Office, Party, and Platform (10/12/26 to 10/25/26) Other includes network cable, radio, satellite, and network broadcast CTV includes spending on streaming, CTV devices and in-app ads. Digital includes spending on Meta, Google, Snapchat, and X CITE SOURCE OF DATA AS: AdImpact with analysis by the Wesleyan Media Project. In the last two weeks, for the period covering October 12 to 25, ad sponsors have spent $1.4 billion (Table 6) on political ads. In prior reports, we tended to find about $1 billion in ad spending every two weeks (since the end of the summer). The spike in ad spending toward the end of the campaigns is typical as ad sponsors make their final pitches to voters. In this most recent two-week timeframe, the share of spending on broadcast television outpaces the full cycle, with about 64 percent of spending on broadcast stations compared to 53 percent in the full cycle. Outside groups remain highly active, out-spending candidates in Senate races and House races by 3:1. Candidates have greatly out-spent groups in gubernatorial campaigns, however. Table 6: Spending by Office, Sponsor Type and Media Channel (10/12/26 to 10/25/26) Office Advertiser Type Local Broadcast (in $Ms) CTV (in $Ms) Digital (in $Ms) Local Cable (in $Ms) Other* (in $Ms) Total (in $Ms) Governor Candidate 104.47 46.26 17.31 12.9 5.73 186.67 Group 53.91 21.83 6.68 8.42 2.52 9