      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "How NFL Owners Spent in 2026 Midterm Elections",
      "pageDescription": "NFL owners put more than $20 million into the midterms, with nearly all of it going to the GOP.",
      "evidenceText": "How NFL Owners Spent in 2026 Midterm Elections Tuesday, November 10, 2026 More Sign In Sign Up Sports Football Basketball Baseball Hockey Soccer Women’s Sports College Sports More Sports Golf Tennis Motorsports Combat Sports Industries Media Finance Stadiums & Real Estate Sports Betting Law Retail Franchises Asset Class Tuned In Explore About Company News Studios Submit a Tip Advertise Contact Careers Shop Politics NFL Owners Shower Republicans With Millions in Midterms Cash Owners put more than $20 million into the midterms, with nearly all of it going to the GOP. By Dennis Young and Margaret Fleming Oct 28, 2026 | 08:00 am UPDATED Nov 02, 2026 | 02:55 pm Ron Chenoy-Imagn Images Want more from Front Office Sports in your search results? Add Front Office Sports on Google This is part of a Front Office Sports series about sports spending in the 2026 midterm elections. NFL owners have spent more than $20 million on candidates and committees in the 2026 midterm elections, Federal Election Commission records show. Nearly all of it has gone to Republicans. That’s not a surprise in the staid NFL, where conservative billionaires have long been in charge. Perhaps more surprising this election cycle is two owners giving heavily to liberal candidates: New Seahawks owner Vinod Khosla donated $2 million to the Democrats’ Senate arm, while Falcons owner Arthur Blank also spent $300,000 trying to help Democrats retake the upper chamber. Blank, in an extreme rarity for an NFL owner, also contributed to the campaign of a self-described democratic socialist. He gave $3,500 to New York Democrat Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, who is running for her fifth term in the House and is widely seen as a 2028 presidential contender. A spokesperson for Blank confirmed his donations but otherwise declined to comment. Both Khosla and Blank have donated to a single Republican each. Khosla, curiously, gave $5,000 to Chuck Fleischmann’s reelection campaign in June; Fleischmann is a Republican who has represented Tennessee in Congress since 2011. Khosla has almost exclusively given to Democrats otherwise. Blank, the Home Depot billionaire who bought the Falcons in 2002, also gave $3,500 to Jay Feely’s campaign. Feely kicked in the NFL for 14 years—including four seasons in Atlanta—and is running as a Republican for a House seat in Arizona. (A wide group of sports figures have donated to Feely in his race.) But Federal Election Commission records show that outside of Khosla and Blank, nearly every NFL dollar spent on Tuesday’s races went toward Republicans, led by the Walton-Penner family who owns the Broncos. Greg Penner, who is the CEO of the team and chairman of Walmart, gave $349,000, with most of it going to Great Opportunity PAC, a committee formed to back Tim Scott’s campaign in the Republican presidential primary that pivoted to outreach to Black and Hispanic voters for Donald Trump. Penner is married to Carrie Walton Penner, the granddaughter of Walmart founder Sam Walton and the daughter of Broncos co-owner Rob Walton. The elder Walton has been a major Republican donor in this cycle, as he has been in past elections. In all, he has spent more than $11 million backing Republican candidates and committees, with $7 million of that going to the Koch-backed Americans for Prosperity, Inc., which has spent millions boosting Republican candidates in Senate battlegrounds. In addition to Walton and Khosla—whose spokespeople did not respond to requests for comment—two other NFL owners spent more than a million dollars on this year’s races. Browns owners Jimmy and Dee Haslam have each spent heavily backing various Republican candidates and committees. Both donated to a group backing Ohio Republican Max Miller, whose ex-wife has accused him of domestic and child abuse amid a bitter custody fight; Miller denies the allegations. Jimmy Haslam gave $100,000 to the Republican National Committee and $75,000 to the National Republican Senatorial Committee, plus much smaller donations to GOP Senate candidates Dan Sullivan, Susan Collins, Jon Husted, Mike Rogers, and others. He also backed Texas Sen. John Cornyn in his primary loss to Ken Paxton. Cowboys owner Jerry Jones, a longtime Republican donor, gave $1 million to Trump’s MAGA Inc., and another half million in Texas races, including $250,000 to a PAC supporting Paxton in a tight Senate race against Democrat James Talarico.  Jones’s son Jerry Jones Jr. gave $50,000 to Paxton’s primary opponent, Cornyn, and $100,000 to Sen. Ten Cruz’s political action committee. The younger Jones gave more than $300,000 in total to Republicans, both in Texas and nationally. Owners of more than half the teams in the NFL chipped in to the league’s Gridiron PAC, which spent more than $700,000 backing candidates and groups on both sides of the aisle. League commissioner Roger Goodell was one of many, many NFL figures who gave to Jay Feely’s committee; he gave the ex-kicker and Republican $7,000 in his race. Dolphins owner Stephen Ross and Patriots owner Robert Kraft, prolific donors who have both thrown major events for President Trump in the past, made big donations to groups supporting Republicans in Senate races across the country. Ross made two $100,000 donations to PACs backing Republicans in Senate races in South Carolina and Florida, while Kraft gave $100,000 in September to the Great Lakes Conservative Fund, a committee that is spending millions— recently in concert with AIPAC —to elect Republican Mike Rogers in Michigan’s Senate race. Kraft has often supported Democrats, but he has long been a backer of Israel, which has emerged as a major issue in the Michigan race. Democrat Abdul El-Sayed has campaigned there on cutting off U.S. military funding to the country. While both men gave to a number of other Republican candidates and groups, each also backed a Democrat. Ross contributed $10,500 to a PAC tied to Sen. Mark Warner (D., Va.), and Kraft gave $27,000 to a group affiliated with Rep. Ritchie Torres (D., N.Y.). This series was reported and produced by Amanda Christovich , Margaret Fleming , Eli Kronenberg , Katie Krzaczek , and Dennis Young . Note: An earlier version of this story attributed $9 million in spending to Broncos owner Rob Walton. The complete figure is more than $11 million. Download the FOS App The business of sports, in your pocket. Scan the QR code to get the app now. Share Tweet Facebook Linkedin Whatsapp Email Copy Link Link Copied Share Link Copied Matt Iseman takes viewers beyond the scoreboard, inside the deals, and face-to-face with the personalities shaping sports. Find Your Local Channel Latest Stories 1 hour ago Are the USMNT and Pochettino Headed For A $10 Million Breakup? Pochettino could take over flailing Manchester United. 2 hours ago 10 Burning Questions About Dianna Russini’s Sports-Media Comeback Plenty of questions remain ahead of Russini’s upcoming return. 3 hours ago Tennessee Men’s Basketball Players Propose CBA The agreement isn’t finalized but serves as a model for future negotiations. What to Read by Michael McCarthy , Ryan Glasspiegel , and Mark Maske 10 Burning Questions About Dianna Russini’s Sports-Media Comeback Plenty of questions remain ahead of Russini’s upcoming return. by Amanda Christovich Tennessee Men’s Basketball Players Propose CBA The agreement isn’t finalized but serves as a model for future negotiations. by Eric Fisher ‘We Believe in Sports’: Skydance Combining HBO Max, Paramount+ Those services have rights to the NFL, MLB, NHL, NASCAR, and more. by Margaret Fleming Stadium Issues Pile Up for NWSL Semifinals It’s déjà vu for women’s sports fans. Front Office Sports Today 11/10/26 – NFL Trade Deadline Day, Russini Speaks, CFP Doubles to 24 1x 0:00 Featured Today by Eli Kronenberg Can MLS Capitalize on Son-Mania? Korea and Tottenham fans alike have flocked to LAFC’s away matches. November 4, 2026 High School Athletic Directors Say They’re Running ‘Mini Colleges’ ADs are managing a growing list of demands from athletes and parents. October 30, 2026 How Injuries Hit Pro Marathoners in the Wallet Staying healthy can get expensive—quickly. October 21, 2026 Die-Hard Sports Fandom Has Reached the Afterlife Will fans pay $6,000 to memorialize their team loyalty? October 20, 2026 Why Europe Is Pumping Out NBA Superstars The NBA and NCAA have an increasingly European flavor. More in Politics by Mark Maske UFC’s Dana White Offered Press Secretary Job By Trump The UFC chief has not publicly accepted the president’s offer. November 4, 2026 Michele Tafoya, Jay Feely Lose Races for Congress Former MLB All-Star Mark Teixeira was elected to Congress. November 4, 2026 Sports Owners Got Very Little for Their Money in Midterm Elections Democrats will hold at least 54 Senate seats. Sponsored How Jeremy Bloom Is Reinventing the X Games Jeremy Bloom discusses transforming the X Games into a global brand. November 3, 2026 Pro Sports Owners Back GOP in Senate Battleground Races Nine key races Tuesday will decide control of the Senate. November 3, 2026 Prediction Markets Could Face Reckoning After Midterms Experts say the industry will face bipartisan pressure regardless of the election’s outcome. November 2, 2026 At Least 10 Sports Owners Backed Mike Rogers in Michigan Senate Race Meanwhile, Chicago Stars minority owner Jennifer Pritzker backed Abdul El-Sayed. October 28, 2026 Mavs’ Miriam Adelson Leads All Sports Owners in Midterms Spending Adelson spent at least $67 million on Republican races. Privacy Manager",
