      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Abdul El-Sayed defeats Mike Rogers despite Haley Stevens drama - Salon.com",
      "pageDescription": "The race capped off a massive night for progressives.",
      "evidenceText": "Abdul El-Sayed defeats Mike Rogers despite Haley Stevens drama - Salon.com Abdul El-Sayed defeats Mike Rogers despite Haley Stevens drama The race capped off a massive night for progressives By CK Smith Weekend Editor Published November 4, 2026 9:19AM (EST) Dr. Abdul El-Sayed waves to a crowd while visiting a polling precinct on the University of Michigan campus in Ann Arbor, Michigan, on Tuesday, Nov. 3, 2026. (Salwan Georges / Getty Images) Facebook X Reddit Email Democrat Abdul El-Sayed is projected to defeat Republican Mike Rogers for Michigan ‘s open U.S. Senate seat, a closely watched race that capped off a massive night for progressives. The Associated Press called the race shortly after 2 a.m. Wednesday morning with El-Sayed leading by about seven points. The candidates were competing to replace retiring Democratic Sen. Gary Peters, whose departure created one of the most consequential open-seat contests of the midterm elections. El-Sayed, a former Detroit health director, campaigned on expanding access to health care and lowering costs, while Rogers, a former congressman and House Intelligence Committee chairman, emphasized manufacturing jobs, tax cuts and his alignment with President Donald Trump. The race took an unexpected turn just two days before Election Day when Democratic Rep. Haley Stevens , who narrowly lost the party’s August primary to El-Sayed, endorsed Rogers during an appearance at a Detroit church. The endorsement stunned Michigan Democrats, prompting condemnation from party leaders and resignations among Stevens’ staff. Stevens cited disagreements with El-Sayed over Israel and his campaign’s direction, while Democrats accused her of undermining their nominee in a critical Senate contest. El-Sayed sought to move past the controversy, urging supporters to focus on voting. His campaign reported raising $2.4 million on Sunday, while Michigan Democratic officials said the endorsement had prompted an increase in volunteers. Rogers, meanwhile, highlighted Stevens’ support in the campaign’s final days. More than 2.3 million Michigan voters had already cast ballots by Monday, according to The Associated Press, raising questions about how much the late-breaking controversy could influence the outcome. Read more about this topic Haley Stevens backs Mike Rogers over fellow Democrat Abdul El-Sayed Obama warns of division as Trump attacks Muslim Senate candidate Abdul El-Sayed is the Democrat who keeps dominating Fox News By CK Smith Related Topics ------------------------------------------ Abdul El-Sayed Donald Trump Haley Stevens Michigan Mike Rogers Related Articles This is an alert message. OK",
