and speech. • Immediately inform Poynter CFO/COO Jessi Navarro in person, by phone (727-337-7131) or email (jnavarro@poynter.org) if you observe or experience unwelcome and offensive behavior. • Seek permission from anyone involved in a program before quoting them or sharing materials that may have been presented. • Some Poynter events are shared with members of the public; please be respectful to all patrons and staff. Unacceptable Behavior Unacceptable behavior includes, but is not limited to, intimidating, threatening, harassing, abusive, discriminatory, derogatory, demeaning or disorderly conduct; or possession, use or distribution of any illegal substances. Poynter does not tolerate the use or abuse of illegal substances at any of the venues where Poynter events are held. Harassing conduct can take many forms. Sexually harassing conduct can be by a person of the same or opposite sex. Poynter reserves the right in its sole discretion to determine what constitutes unacceptable behavior. Consequences of Unacceptable Behavior Unacceptable behavior will not be tolerated whether by other attendees, media, speakers or venue staff. Anyone asked to stop engaging in unacceptable behavior is expected to comply immediately. If a participant engages in unacceptable behavior, Poynter may take any action it deems appropriate, up to and including immediate expulsion without warning from the event and without refund of any associated fees. What to Do if You Witness or Are Subject to Unacceptable Behavior Harassment will not be tolerated by Poynter. If you are subject to unacceptable behavior, notice that someone else is being subject to unacceptable behavior or have any other concerns, please immediately notify security staff and/or Poynter CFO/COO Jessi Navarro in person, by phone (727-337-7131) or e-mail (jnavarro@poynter.org). Also notify appropriate law enforcement if necessary. Poynter will be available to help participants contact venue security or local law enforcement, to provide escorts, or to otherwise assist those experiencing unacceptable behavior to feel safe for the duration of the event. https://www.poynter.org/poynter-institute-code-ethics/#conduct I certify I have read and understand Poynter's Code of Conduct listed above. Thank you for your application. It will be processed once you press \"submit.\" After the application deadline, we will go through a period of review. No payment is necessary at this time. We will contact you with further details if you are accepted. A copy of this application will be emailed to you as confirmation. Δ Sign In Register Sign into your account Sign In or Register to Enroll - OR - Checkout as a Guest Remember Me Reset Password Don't have an account? Register Sign In Register Register a new account Sign In or Register to Enroll Password should contain at least: Eight characters One uppercase letter One lowercase letter One number One of the following special characters: @ # - _ $ * % ^ & + = § ! ? Already have an account? Sign In Sign In Register Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) $ 1,550.00 Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) quantity Apply Now Leadership Reporting & Editing St. Petersburg, Fla. Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) Apply by Dec. 7, 2026, for a five-day, in-person program designed for new managers ready to build the practical skills and confidence to lead people and teams effectively. Through hands-on training, personalized feedback and peer learning, you’ll practice essential skills for delegation, coaching, difficult conversations and managing up. This program takes place March 1–5, 2027, at Poynter’s campus in St. Petersburg, Florida. March 1, 2027 &ndash; March 5, 2027 Overview Submit your application by Dec. 7, 2026. Meet March 1–5, 2027, for five days of in-person training at Poynter’s campus in St. Petersburg, Florida. Learn directly from Poynter faculty Tony Elkins and Fernanda Camarena. Discover and leverage your unique strengths with a Gallup CliftonStrengths report. Practice real-world management skills through hands-on exercises, peer discussions and coaching. Work through the challenges of transitioning from colleague to manager alongside peers navigating similar experiences. Get 360-degree feedback from your direct reports, supervisor and peers with Poynter’s ViewPoynt tool. Build relationships with a cohort of new managers through an immersive, transformational leadership experience. Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) quantity Apply Now SKU: 82704 Tags: At Poynter , Fernanda Camarena , front page course , In-person , Leadership , Tony Elkins Learning Outcomes In this interactive, five-day workshop, you will: Understand your strengths and how they shape your approach to leadership. Learn how to delegate effectively and set clear expectations. Give constructive feedback and coach employees. Gain strategies for navigating difficult workplace conversations. Learn how to onboard, develop and retain talented employees. Apply strategies for managing up and building stronger working relationships. Understand how to lead distributed teams with different work styles and needs. Develop strategies for managing your workload more sustainably. Apply by Dec 7, 2026 $ 1,550.00 Essential Skills for New Managers (March 2027) quantity Apply Now Overview Submit your application by Dec. 7, 2026. Meet March 1–5, 2027, for five days of in-person training at Poynter’s campus in St. Petersburg, Florida. Learn directly from Poynter faculty Tony Elkins and Fernanda Camarena. Discover and leverage your unique strengths with a Gallup CliftonStrengths report. Practice real-world management skills through hands-on exercises, peer discussions and coaching. Work through the challenges of transitioning from colleague to manager alongside peers navigating similar experiences. Get 360-degree feedback from your direct reports, supervisor and peers with Poynter’s ViewPoynt tool. Build relationships with a cohort of new managers through an immersive, transformational leadership experience. Training 5 or More People? get consulting & coaching Description Schedule Requirements Essential Skills for New Managers is for people with three or fewer years of management experience who want to build the practical skills and confidence to lead people and teams effectively. If you’re navigating the transition from colleague to manager, learning how to delegate or figuring out how to give feedback and handle difficult conversations, this program is designed for you. Apply if you’re new to managing people Over five days at Poynter’s campus in St. Petersburg, Florida, you’ll build practical skills to delegate effectively, give feedback and coaching, manage up, navigate challenging conversations and lead different types of teams. You’ll also gain a better understanding of your own strengths and leadership style through Poynter’s signature 360-degree feedback tool, the ViewPoynt, personalized small-group coaching and a Gallup CliftonStrengths assessment. You’ll put what you learn into practice through exercises, peer discussions and small-group coaching, with opportunities to work through real management challenges you’re facing. This in-person program will be offered twice in 2027: March 1–5 and Nov. 8–12.   Why learn leadership with Poynter? The Poynter Institute is a global nonprofit working to strengthen journalism to help address society’s most pressing problems. The world’s top journalists and emerging media leaders rely on the Institute to learn new skills, adopt best practices, better serve audiences, scale operations and foster trustworthy information. Poynter’s leadership training draws on more than 50 years of experience strengthening leaders in media. Our faculty bring deep expertise in management, leadership, individual coaching, self-assessment frameworks, conflict management and more. Every program emphasizes practical, hands-on learning you can put to work immediately, along with intentional cohort building that creates space for meaningful connection and shared experience.   Essential Skills is part of a broader Poynter leadership track, with programs for different stages and types of leadership in media. Whether you’re leading a project without formal authority, managing people for the first time or taking on greater organizational responsibility, there’s a program designed to help you grow. The leadership track: Lead with Influence : Lead projects and influence others without formal authority; no formal management experience required, happens in February (apply by Jan. 8, 2027) and September (apply by July 9, 2027) Essential Skills for New Managers : Build the fundamentals of managing people; 0-3 years of management experience, happens in March (apply by Dec. 7, 2026) and November (apply by Aug. 16, 2027) Poynter Leadership Academy : Strengthen your skills as an experienced or senior leader; 3+ years of leadership/management experience (apply by Jan. 18, 2027) Leadership Academy for Women : Advanced leadership development centered on the experiences of women; 3+ years of leadership/management experience (apply by Feb. 8, 2027) Questions? If you need assistance, email us at info@poynter.org . This in-person-only program takes place March 1-5, 2027, in St. Petersburg, Florida, at the Poynter Institute. Sessions will take place from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. Eastern time most days. Lunch, refreshments and light snacks are provided during the program. Accepted applicants must plan to arrive on site no later than the start date and time of Essential Skills (no late arrivals), and should expect to dedicate uninterrupted time to the program for its duration. Below is a tentative schedule, subject to change: Day 1 | Learning About Your Management Style and Focusing on Strengths Start by exploring who you are as a manager. You’ll examine your leadership style, dig into your Gallup CliftonStrengths results and identify the strengths you can leverage as you grow as a leader. Day 2 | ",
